Thanks so much for having me.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.080
I loved your book.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:42.940
It was so enlightening and scary

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.020
and funny all at the same time.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:47.660
Thank you so much.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:50.060
There are people who are having difficulty with this name.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.900
K-I-R-I-A-K-O-O.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:54.740
Is that how you-

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.060
O-U.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:57.380
O-U.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.460
Yeah, even I can't get it right.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.500
So if they're going to go to your site,

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.700
then it's just John Carriaco.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:06.300
K-O-U at the end.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.460
A-K-I-R-I-A-K-O-U.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.380
And there are some commentaries there

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:17.380
and even in the book that are so heartfelt

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.580
but scary.

00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:24.900
And one of the things,

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:28.280
the most memorable thing in your book to me

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.500
was this man that they went after

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.100
and he refused to testify.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:38.740
So they threatened his grandmother and his son

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:42.180
because they lived in the same house

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:47.180
and made him bow down by threatening his family.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:49.180
How would you tell that story

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.740
and how often is that used?

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:55.300
Oh yes, to answer your second question first,

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.100
this is used every single day

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.300
in the American judicial system.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.820
And this is part of the reason why

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:05.780
the federal government wins 98.2% of its cases

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:07.420
according to ProPublica.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.420
But this was a cellmate of mine

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:15.300
who to make a long story short

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:20.420
had inadvertently accessed a secure government database.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:23.540
When I say inadvertently,

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:27.180
I mean because he had won a government contract

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.620
but his personnel officer had forgotten

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:31.940
to put him on the security list.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:33.860
But when he called to get the password,

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:35.460
he was given the password.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:39.820
So they charged him with unauthorized access

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:40.900
to a secure database,

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:42.900
which is a felony, believe it or not.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.220
Well, he said, listen, this was a mistake.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:49.340
I thought my security officer had put me on the contract.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.140
It was an oversight.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:54.220
And I didn't do anything wrong.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:57.780
They said, well, we're offering you nine months in prison.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:01.260
You're in, you're out, you never do it again.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.700
And he said, no, because if he took a guilty plea,

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.140
he would lose his security clearance

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:09.220
and really lose his ability to make a living.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:13.260
Well, he so angered them by not taking a plea

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.580
that they started digging into his past.

00:17:16.580 --> 00:17:19.860
And what they found was that he and his wife

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:24.300
had gone to a car dealership a couple of years before

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.420
because his wife needed a new car.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.380
She picked the car she wanted.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.820
She wanted it in a different color.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.660
So instead of buying it off the lot, they ordered it.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:36.500
In the meantime, he and his wife owned a home in England.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:37.780
She was British.

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:39.700
And so she went back to England

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.220
to get the house ready for the summer.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:46.180
In the meantime, the car dealer calls his name is Dave

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:47.580
and says, your car's ready.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:49.220
He goes to pick it up.

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:50.700
He signs the contract.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:51.820
They said, where's your wife?

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:53.860
She needs to sign the contract too.

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:55.540
He said, she's in England.

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:56.820
The dealer said, that's okay.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.100
Just sign her name here.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:17:59.660
So he signed her name.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:03.140
So they charged him with one count of mail,

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:06.140
I'm sorry, of wire fraud for signing his wife's name

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.020
and two counts of aggravated identity theft.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:13.940
And then because the car came with a $1,500 rebate,

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:16.940
they also charged him with wire fraud.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:21.500
So he had been looking at nine months in prison.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:24.820
He ended up taking a plea to nine years

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.580
and four months in prison because they said,

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:30.540
finally he kept refusing a deal.

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:31.700
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:33.060
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:36.100
So finally they said, well, your grandmother

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.260
and your 16 year old daughter were in the house

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.500
when you access the database.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.140
We're gonna charge them with being accomplices

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:44.420
to accessing the database

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:46.460
and we'll charge them with conspiracy

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:47.980
if you don't take a plea.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:49.460
And so he took a plea.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:52.100
And that's how our government works.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:54.420
They do these two other things.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.580
One is called charge stacking.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.700
Let's say maybe you really do something wrong.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.300
You really do commit a crime.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:05.100
Instead of just charging you with that crime,

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:09.180
they'll charge you with five, 10 felonies, right?

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.380
And they'll add conspiracy and making a false statement

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.300
and wire fraud and mail fraud

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:18.060
and all these what are called throw away charges.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.900
And then they'll make you defend yourself.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:23.260
And after you finally go bankrupt,

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.580
because believe me, you're gonna go bankrupt

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:28.140
having to defend yourself against all these charges.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:30.060
They'll come to you and they'll say,

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:31.940
all right, we wanna offer you a deal.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:34.020
We'll dismiss all of the charges

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.420
but one, if you take a guilty plea.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.500
Well, of course you're gonna take a guilty plea

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:39.980
because if you go to trial,

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:41.780
yeah, you know, juries in this country,

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.580
they're gonna find a baloney sandwich guilty.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:48.140
So, do you take the deal?

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:51.420
Or do you risk winning, you know,

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.460
knowing that you have a 1.8% chance of winning

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:57.100
and if you lose, you're gonna spend 20, 30,

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:58.860
40 years in prison.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:00.100
That's charged.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.600
Another thing they do is called venue shopping.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:10.000
In my case, and in the cases of other whistleblowers like Jeffrey Sterling, for example, from the

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:14.760
CIA, or even Edward Snowden, we're all charged in the Eastern District of Virginia.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.880
You don't even have to ever have been in the Eastern District of Virginia.

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:21.920
But the Eastern District of Virginia is known as the Espionage Court, because no national

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.160
security defendant has ever won a case there.

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:28.520
And so that's where they charge all the national security cases.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:37.080
If there's a judge, for example, who's particularly tough on bank robbers, they'll charge you with

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:41.480
bank robbery in his or her district.

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.040
So that's venue shopping.

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:47.080
They charge you in the district where they know they're going to get a guilty verdict

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.560
and they know they're going to get the stiffest sentence.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:51.960
And that's not justice.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:53.960
That's politics.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:56.200
Wow.

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:57.200
I read that.

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:00.280
I read that part and it blew my mind then, and here you spell it out.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:03.640
It's like just appalling.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:09.880
But I want to redirect slightly for people who aren't familiar with your story.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:13.720
What was your journey to becoming a counterterrorism expert?

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:20.040
I mean, from that story, you sound like a whistleblower, and I mean that in the

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.320
most positive of ways.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:29.800
But you really went in and were dedicated to the CIA and highly trained, I mean like highly

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:30.800
trained.

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:31.800
Highly trained.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:35.040
You sound like a really nice, easy going guy.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:36.040
Let's have a beer.

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.040
But I mean...

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:38.680
Yeah, that's all part of the job.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.920
You have to love people and you have to love being around people.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:47.360
Even bad people, because your job is to recruit spies to steal secrets.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:54.480
And then to use those secrets so that the CIA and analysts can analyze them and provide the

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:59.000
policymakers with the top finished intelligence possible.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:04.560
So you really have to ingratiate yourself with people to get them to trust you and to like

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:09.120
you to the point where they're willing to commit treason for you in some cases or

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.600
to commit espionage for you.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:12.680
Wow.

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:13.680
And did you...

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.080
Were you always inclined this way?

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:20.440
I mean were you affable before you got into being trained by the CIA?

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:22.960
Were you always this personality and they just took advantage of it?

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:23.960
Oh yeah.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:25.600
I like to think my personality hasn't changed.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.440
I really do love being around people.

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:29.080
I love talking to people.

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:33.400
I draw strength from being in groups of people.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:34.800
I've always loved it.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:39.120
When I was in graduate school, I went to George Washington University in Washington

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:42.400
D.C. for both my undergrad and graduate degrees.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:47.200
I got a bachelor's degree in Middle Eastern studies and then I stated GW to work on a master's

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:51.720
degree in legislative affairs but with a focus on foreign policy analysis.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:55.200
And I was taking a class called the psychology of leadership.

00:22:55.200 --> 00:23:01.440
And it was being taught by an eminent psychiatrist who's still around in practicing Dr. Gerald

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:02.440
Post.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:07.800
He had a PhD in political science, a PhD in psychology and an MD.

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:11.880
And he assigned us a fascinating paper one day.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:17.880
We were supposed to shadow our bosses and write a psychological profile on our bosses.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:22.440
At the time I was working for a labor union, the United Food and Commercial Workers Union

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.440
at their international headquarters here in Washington.

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:30.000
And I worked for a guy who I was a little bit afraid of.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.000
He was one of these old school union organizers.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:42.800
He had had his back broken during a strike one time, just a big, mean, tough guy.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.560
And we didn't necessarily get along very well.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:50.920
He wasn't really my cup of tea, but I was shadowing him for the week.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:54.560
And then halfway through the week, we had an argument.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:57.600
And I called him a racist, which he was.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:02.720
And he got so angry, his face got red and he bawled up his fist.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:07.840
And I remember putting my hands up and recoiling a little because I thought, well, for sure

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.080
here it comes, he's going to punch me.

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:15.240
And instead he exploded and he shouted, my penis is bigger than yours.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:20.560
And I said, you're nuts, and I quit, and I walked out.

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:24.080
And I wrote my paper and I did not say in the paper that I had quit, but I said

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:29.720
that my conclusion was that he was a sociopath with psychopathic tendencies and he was

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:31.920
potentially violent.

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:35.240
So a week later, I get the paper back.

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:37.920
And my professor had written a note in the column.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:42.920
He gave me an A and he wrote, I've never done this before, but I urge you to quit this job.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:48.040
I agree with your assessment that your boss is a sociopath with psychopathic and violent

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.040
tendencies.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.560
Please see me after class.

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:54.760
So I went up to him after class and I said, Dr. Post, did you want to see me?

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:56.680
Yes, he says, come to my office.

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:01.800
So I go to the office, he closes the door and he says, look, I'm not really a professor

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:02.680
here.

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:07.280
I'm a CIA officer undercover as a professor here.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:11.400
And I'm looking for people who might fit into the CIA's culture.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.220
I think you would fit into the CIA's culture.

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:16.600
Would you like to work for the CIA?

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:18.520
Now of course, that's illegal today.

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:22.240
We have equal employment opportunity laws in this country.

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:25.840
But this is the proverbial old boy network.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:29.160
And I said, yeah, I'd love to work for the CIA.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:36.520
And so I started going through the motions of a battery of tests, written, psychological,

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:41.440
medical, polygraph, comprehensive background investigation.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:45.760
And eight months later, I was working at the CIA.

00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:48.760
Wow.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:50.760
And what happened to the legislative part?

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:54.000
Is that sort of, do you think that's part of your background too?

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:59.360
I mean, you have, you seem, and from your story, we will get into talking about prison.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:05.600
But I mean, you seem like a kind of a rabble-rousing organizer, even though you have this affable,

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:10.240
you know, nice guy, but I mean, you do have that undertone of, you know, you seem like

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:13.560
you want to be a changemaker and have people pay attention to truth.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:18.920
Well, my wife says that I never saw a bridge I didn't want to burn, which I think is

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:20.420
not entirely fair.

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:27.700
But you know, the older I get, I do tend to see more things in terms of black and white,

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:28.700
sure.

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:32.460
At the CIA, we're taught that everything is a shade of gray.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:34.860
And I've concluded that that's just simply not true.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:36.980
Some things are right and wrong.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:39.180
And in my case, torture was wrong.

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:40.180
It was wrong.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:41.340
It was illegal.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:44.140
And it was morally and ethically wrong.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:47.900
And I decided to take a stand on it.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:53.660
And when did torture float up into your view?

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:58.980
I mean, how did that become, again, for the backstory for people, this was part of, you

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:04.940
know, this is why you went to jail was because they, as I will say, you'll have different

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:10.340
ways of saying it, you know, they came after you for like disclosing this information

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:13.060
about torture that you had a position.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:16.540
When did that float into your view and why and why did you take such a strong

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:17.540
position?

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:23.620
Well, in the aftermath of the 9-11 attacks, I became the CIA's chief of counterterrorism

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:25.860
operations in Pakistan.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:31.180
And in that job, I led a series of raids in March of 2002 that resulted in the capture

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.980
of Abu Zubaydah.

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:37.340
And I'm not allowed to say the number, but many dozens of al-Qaeda fighters.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.420
Abu Zubaydah, we believed at the time, was the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:47.020
And this was the highest-ranking terrorist capture that we had ever made to that

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:48.100
point.

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:53.140
So I finished that tour in the early summer of 2002.

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:57.980
I went back to headquarters and a senior officer in the CIA's Counterterrorism

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:01.700
Center asked me if I wanted to be what he called certified in the use of

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:03.500
enhanced interrogation techniques.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:06.100
I had never heard the term before.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:07.860
So I asked him what he meant.

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:09.540
He explained what these techniques were.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:13.220
And I said, wow, I said, that sounds like a torture program to me.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:14.940
I don't think I'm interested.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:17.180
But let me think about it for an hour.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:21.100
So I went up to the CIA's executive floor, the seventh floor.

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:25.780
And there was a very, very senior CIA officer for whom I had worked in the

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:27.180
Middle East about a decade earlier.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:29.300
And I went to ask him his advice.

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:32.860
And he said, first, this is a torture program.

00:28:32.860 --> 00:28:36.740
They can use whatever euphemism they want, but this is a torture program.

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:39.660
Secondly, torture is a slippery slope.

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:41.740
And you know how some of these guys are.

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.380
Somebody's going to go overboard and they're going to kill a prisoner.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:47.420
And when that happens, there's going to be a congressional investigation,

00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:50.220
and then there's going to be a justice department investigation.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:51.860
And somebody's going to go to prison.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.060
Do you want to go to prison?

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:55.300
And I said, no, I don't want to go to prison.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:57.860
